  "response": "## The bug\n\n**Finding:** `crime_00009` — Timezone Roulette at `src/date.ts:29,33`.\n\nTwo literals are flagged, but only one is the culprit:\n\n- **`\"2026-12-25T07:00:00\"`** (line 29) — datetime with **no** timezone marker. Per ECMAScript, a date-time string without `Z`/offset is parsed in the **host's local zone**. In CI (UTC server) local == UTC, so it lands at `07:00Z`. In production for a European user (UTC+1/+2), the same literal is parsed as local Europe time = `05:00–06:00Z` — i.e. the promo's \"open\" instant moves earlier in absolute time, and a downstream date-rounded comparison flips the calendar day forward, so the window appears open \"a day early.\"\n- **`\"2026-12-20\"`** (line 33) — bare date strings are *always* parsed as UTC midnight by spec, so this one is consistent across CI and production. Still flagged because it's ambiguous to readers, but it isn't the bug.\n\nThe literal responsible is **`\"2026-12-25T07:00:00\"`**.\n\n## Smallest change\n\nAppend `Z` to anchor the literal to UTC:\n\n```diff\n- new Date(\"2026-12-25T07:00:00\")\n+ new Date(\"2026-12-25T07:00:00Z\")\n```\n\nThat's it — one character. Production parses the same instant as CI did, the function keeps its name, and no other code has to move. The deeper fixes the scan suggests (inject a clock per `crime_00002`, unify UTC/local methods per `crime_00003`, switch to a TZ-aware lib per `crime_00011`) are separate cleanups; none are needed to make the failing production behaviour match the green CI behaviour.",
